  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish that processing rate, annealing, pressure, and composition can control phase coexistence in HEAs; that FCC or BCC matrices can coexist with intermetallic or C14 Laves phases; and that compositional substitution or two-phase decomposition can reduce saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. R13 and R17 also connect arc melting or fast cooling with phase selection. However, none describes rapidly quenched FeCoNiTi, an FCC+C14 microstructure in that system, a composition-matched C14-dominant control, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, or orbital/spin-canting-assisted domain-wall pinning. Thus the literature points broadly toward processing-controlled multiphase magnetic HEAs but leaves the specific intervention and mechanism as a substantial new idea."
}
